! An additive stress and an eigenstrain -- "Stress Load" and "Strain Load" --
! through ElasticSolve, which had neither until the affine offset channel was
! added to its LocalMatrix. Together the two keywords make the constitutive
! response affine rather than linear:
!
!   sigma = C : ( eps - "Strain Load" ) + "Stress Load"
!
! and that is StressSolve's reading of them, which this test pins ElasticSolve to.
!
! NOTHING IN THE TREE COVERED THESE KEYWORDS ON EITHER SOLVER. A grep over every
! sif in fem/tests found no "Stress Load", no "Strain Load", no "Pre Stress" and
! no "Pre Strain", so StressSolve's implementation -- the reference for the port --
! was itself unverified, and the first run of it in Elmer's history is this test
! and its sibling StressStressLoad, which is the same case through StressSolve.
!
! WHY THIS NEEDS NO NEW ASSEMBLY ROUTINE, which is the interesting part. An affine
! law is not linear in its strain measure, and ElasticSolve's LocalMatrix forms its
! tangent by Gateaux derivatives, which requires linearity -- it asserts
! MatModel % StrainLinear for that reason. The offset survives because of WHERE it
! is added: to the residual stress alone, never to a term a derivative is taken
! of. The tangent terms apply the law to strain INCREMENTS, so they exclude the
! offset by construction. Adding it inside the constitutive model instead would
! carry it into the derivative and the stiffness would be wrong.
!
! THE REFERENCE VALUE IS STRESSSOLVE'S, so this single-solver run carries a
! cross-solver assertion. On this problem the two agree on every printed digit of
! "Stress_yz" and "Stress_xx" and to the last digit of the displacement -- field by
! field 2e-14 relative, the displacement control 2e-15.
!
! THE LOAD IS SHEAR DOMINANT AND THE TWO CHANNELS ARE BALANCED, and both of those
! are the point of the test rather than arbitrary. Measured, by mimicking each
! plausible coding error in the sif and comparing the fields:
!
!   error mimicked                                     moves Stress_yz by
!   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
!   eigenstrain shear not halved (engineering vs           87%
!     tensor shear -- the classic Voigt mistake)
!   Voigt slots 5 and 6 exchanged in "Strain Load"         68%
!   Voigt slots 5 and 6 exchanged in "Stress Load"        143%
!   "Stress Load" ignored                                 220%
!   "Strain Load" ignored                                 120%
!   offset added with the wrong sign                      441%
!
! With the more natural choice of a stress load a hundred times larger, the same
! six errors moved the answer by 0.02% to 0.5% -- detectable against a 1e-5
! tolerance but only just, because the shear terms were then a rounding error on
! the normal ones and the two channels were not comparable in size. Hence the
! deliberately shear-heavy, sign-mixed values below, and hence "Stress_yz" as the
! checked component.
!
! Small strain on purpose, so the kinematics reduce to StressSolve's and what is
! compared is the offset channel alone. Under "Large Deflection" the same offset
! becomes a genuine pre-stress -- it reaches the tangent through the geometric
! term, which is the capability StressSolve spells "Pre Stress" -- and that is a
! separate matter from this test.

Header
  Mesh DB "." "cube"
End

Simulation
  Max Output Level = 5
  Coordinate System = Cartesian
  Simulation Type = Steady State
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
  Output Intervals = 0
End

Body 1
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
  Body Force = 1
End

Material 1
  Density = 1.0
  Youngs modulus = 1.0e3
  Poisson ratio = 0.3
End

Body Force 1
  ! Elmer's Voigt order with engineering shear: (11, 22, 33, 12, 23, 13). All six
  ! slots are distinct in size AND in sign, so a permutation cannot cancel, and
  ! the three shear slots are the largest, so a factor of two lost on them cannot
  ! hide behind the normal components.
  !
  ! The two channels are scaled to contribute comparably: C is of order 1e3 and
  ! the eigenstrain of order 1e-3, so C : eps0 is of order 1 and the stress load
  ! is given at order 1 to match.
  Stress Load(6) = Real 1.0 -2.0 0.5 3.0 -5.0 1.5
  Strain Load(6) = Real 1.0e-3 -2.0e-3 0.5e-3 4.0e-3 -6.0e-3 2.0e-3
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers(2) = 1 2
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "Elasticity"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = -dofs 3 Displacement

  Large Deflection = False

  Calculate Stresses = Logical True
  Calculate Strains = Logical True
  Displace Mesh = Logical False

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPack

  ! The offset is affine, so the residual is independent of the iterate and one
  ! Newton step is exact. More than one iteration is allowed here so that a
  ! failure to converge would show as a failure rather than as a truncated solve.
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 12
  Nonlinear System Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-11
  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-9
End

Solver 2
  Equation = "SaveScalars"
  Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
  Exec Solver = After Simulation
  Show Norm Index = 1
  ! The shear component the sensitivity table above is measured on. Stress_xx is
  ! reported alongside it as the normal-component witness: it is blind to both
  ! slot exchanges, which is itself worth seeing in the log.
  Variable 1 = String "Stress_yz"
  Operator 1 = String "max abs"
  Variable 2 = String "Stress_xx"
  Operator 2 = String "max abs"
End

! One face clamped, everything else free: the offset is the only thing loading
! the body, so nothing else can carry the answer.
Boundary Condition 1
  Target Boundaries(1) = 5
  Displacement 1 = Real 0.0
  Displacement 2 = Real 0.0
  Displacement 3 = Real 0.0
End

Solver 2 :: Reference Norm = Real 4.54751054E+00
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1.0e-5
RUN
